  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 AM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe mid/upper level ridge will retrograde and flatten out tomorrow, \nkeeping the MCS activity across the Ohio Valley and Great Lakes. \nThis shift in the ridge should bring in slightly drier air, with PW \nvalues falling to around 1.5 inches. Forecast soundings also \nindicate a stronger cap suggesting less afternoon convective \ndevelopment compared to recent days. A few isolated showers or \nthunderstorms remain possible across southwest VA and the TN \nmountains.\n\nWith fewer clouds and a lower rain chance, diurnal heating will \nallow temperatures to climb into the 90s for most locations. \nAlthough boundary layer mixing should lower afternoon dewpoints \nsomewhat into the lower to middle 70s. Heat Index values of 105+ \ndegrees are still expected across the central and southern TN \nValley. These conditions can lead to heat related illness, \nespecially for those spending extended time outdoors. Limit \nstrenuous activity during the hottest part of the day, drink plenty \nof water, and take frequent breaks in air conditioning or shade.\n\nOn Sunday night, some CAMs indicate remnant MCS convection moving \nacross West Virginia, potentially clipping portions of southwest \nVirginia. Given the overnight timing, the severe threat looks \nlimited.\n\nMonday will see Heat Index values within the ballpark of what we've \nexperienced this weekend. There's a chance that the boundary layer \nmixing on Monday brings in lower dew points and the humidity is not \ntechnically Heat ADvisory criteria... But it's still going to be hot. \nWill go ahead and extend the Heat Advisory through Monday for one \nmore day.\n\nA shortwave trough and surface cold front will move through Kentucky \nMonday morning, sparking off convection as the system approaches the \nPlateau during the afternoon. The air mass ahead of the front should \nremain capped into the early afternoon while helping to increase \ninstability. Some shear will also be present, particularly across \nnorthern TN and southwest VA, with the NAM showing approximately 30-\n40 kt of deep layer shear. An inverted V profile, steep lapse rates, \nand a deep boundary layer would support strong downdrafts and \ndamaging wind gusts. DCAPE values of 1000+ J/kg further support this \nconcern. The RRFS depicts a fairly intense convective line moving \nthrough the area between roughly 7 PM and 11 PM.\n\nGiven these factors, the potential exists for a more significant \nsevere weather event Monday afternoon and evening, with damaging \nstraight line winds being the primary concern. Large hail and an \nisolated tornado cannot be completely ruled out. Those with outdoor \nplans should have a reliable way to monitor changing weather \nconditions.\n\nThe surface front will push through the area Monday night, bringing \nslightly cooler temperatures and lower dewpoints for Tuesday. This \nshould end the dangerous heat potential, although temperatures will \nremain near normal.\n\nThe ridge will continue to retrograde through the remainder of the \nweek, resulting in a broad trough across the eastern CONUS. This \npattern will increase rain chances through the week as embedded \nshortwaves move through the larger scale trough and the jet stream \nsettles into the Ohio Valley. Showers and thunderstorms will \ntherefore remain possible, with locally heavy rainfall and lightning \ncontinuing to be the primary concerns with stronger storms.\n\nOverall, the most notable hazards through the forecast will be \ndangerous heat through Monday, localized flooding from repeated \nthunderstorms, and a potentially more significant severe weather \nthreat Monday afternoon and evening.",
